# GATHER — the two blocks pushed together.
#
# ⚠ THE SAME FAMILY, THE SAME BINARY, A DIFFERENT GOAL AND A DIFFERENT ACTIVE
# SET. That is the claim the task layer exists to make: this file and the two
# beside it produce three different tasks with no .mojo touched between them.
#
# ⚠⚠ THIS TASK'S GOAL USED TO HOLD AT RESET. It was
# `And(On(brick, table_top), On(cube_a, table_top))` with both props sampled
# INTO `table_top` — so the sampler satisfied the goal before the arm moved,
# and a policy could score 100% by doing nothing. `test_task_reset_steps`
# ASSERTED that it held, which is how it survived: the gate read the coincidence
# as a feature (the sampler's z agreeing with `ON_MAX_DZ`) and never asked the
# separate question of whether the TASK was non-trivial. Both questions are
# asked now, and `so101_settle_brick.task` carries the first one on purpose.
schema_version=1
task=so101_gather_bricks
family=so101_tabletop
language=Push the two blocks together

# ⚠⚠ 0.06 IS BELOW THE SAMPLER'S GUARANTEED SEPARATION, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
# DESIGN OF THIS TASK. `table_left` and `table_right` are disjoint strips with
# an 8 cm gutter between them, so two props drawn one from each start at least
# 0.08 apart — FOR EVERY DRAW, not for most of them. A margin that merely
# held on average would make the task degenerate on a fraction of lanes and
# report as a policy that mysteriously starts above zero.
#
# ⚠ AND IT IS ABOVE WHAT THE GEOMETRY ALLOWS. Two 4 cm cubes resting face to
# face have centres 0.04 apart, so 0.06 is reachable with room to spare rather
# than asking for interpenetration.
#
# ⚠ `Near` NAMES NO REGION, which is why this task can use `table_left` and
# `table_right` at all — see the family's note on the one-region device table.
goal=Near(brick, cube_a, 0.06)

active=table
active=brick
active=cube_a

init=brick@table_left
init=cube_a@table_right
